= Sequence alignment =

== Overview ==
'''Sequence alignment''' is the process and resulting arrangement of two or more biological sequences so that comparable positions are placed in correspondence under a scoring or probabilistic model.

Pairwise or multiple alignments can be global, local, semi-global, structure-informed, or specialized for particular evolutionary or biological assumptions. Matches, substitutions, insertions, and deletions contribute to alignment scores.

Alignment supports similarity search, homology analysis, functional inference, structural comparison, phylogenetics, and variant analysis. An alignment is a model of correspondence, not direct proof that positions are homologous or functionally equivalent.

Different scoring matrices, gap penalties, algorithms, sequence quality, and evolutionary divergence can produce different alignments. Multiple-sequence alignment is especially model- and heuristic-dependent for large datasets.

== Boundaries ==
* alignment ≠ proof of homology
* sequence similarity ≠ homology by definition
* highest score ≠ biologically correct alignment under every model

== Related concepts ==
[[Biological sequence]], [[Algorithm]], [[Probability]], [[Uncertainty]], [[Phylogenetics]], and [[Sequence annotation]] provide the comparison context.

== Alignment as a phylogenetic assumption ==
Multiple-sequence alignment is often used before [[Related to::Phylogenetics|phylogenetic inference]] to define comparable molecular positions. This makes alignment uncertainty part of the evolutionary analysis: an incorrect correspondence can bias inferred substitutions and tree structure. Similarity search, homology assessment and phylogenetic reconstruction use related evidence but are not interchangeable operations.

== Reproducibility and interpretation ==
A reported alignment should identify the source sequence accessions, database release, algorithm, scoring model, gap penalties and any filtering or masking. Two methods can produce different, defensible alignments under different biological assumptions. A high score may reflect composition bias or a short local match, so biological inference requires context beyond the score.

